<!--
    `26-34`: the adaptive-icon foreground layer - the brandbook mark's glyph, capital "A", set in
    Unbounded Bold (weight 700), the brand's own open-source (OFL) Google Fonts display face.

    The outline below is a real mechanical extraction, not a hand-drawn approximation:
    `Unbounded-Bold.ttf` was downloaded from Google Fonts' own CDN (the exact static weight-700
    instance `fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Unbounded:wght@700` resolves to:
    fonts.gstatic.com/s/unbounded/v12/Yq6F-LOTXCb04q32xlpat-6uR42XTqtG6__2040.ttf), then the "A"
    glyph's outline was pulled from its `glyf` table with
    `fontTools.pens.svgPathPen.SVGPathPen` (Python, `fontTools` package) - see the commit report
    for the extraction script and the side-by-side render against an independent FreeType-based
    rendering of the same glyph, confirming it is the real letterform, right side up.

    Placement: scaled and centered into the 66x66dp safe zone of this 108x108dp canvas (the safe
    zone spans [21,87] on each axis; content outside it can be clipped by some launcher masks).
    The brandbook's own reference proportion for the web tile (glyph ~60% of the tile's height)
    is applied here to the *safe zone's* own height per this item's note, not the full 108dp:

      target glyph height = 0.60 * 66dp = 39.6dp
      glyph cap height in font units (unitsPerEm=1000) = 750 (bounding box y: 0..750)
      scale = 39.6 / 750 = 0.0528
      glyph bounding box: x: 1..956, y: 0..750 (font units) -> center (478.5, 375)
      canvas center = (54, 54) (same point for the 108x108 canvas and the concentric safe zone)

      for each font-unit point (fx, fy):
        canvas x = 54 + (fx - 478.5) * 0.0528
        canvas y = 54 - (fy - 375)   * 0.0528     // flip: font space is Y-up (baseline at 0,
                                                    // ascender positive), vector space is Y-down

      resulting scaled glyph size on the canvas: ~50.4dp wide x 39.6dp tall, comfortably inside
      the 66x66dp safe zone on both axes.

    The glyph has no curves in its own outline (a geometric/display face - this "A" is built
    entirely from straight edges), so the transformed path below uses only M/L/Z.

    `26-104`: the placement above under-filled the circle - the "A" read as a tiny glyph floating
    in a large disc (notification large icon, launcher, task switcher). The path's own bounding
    box is x:[28.788,79.212] y:[34.200,73.800] (width 50.424dp, height 39.600dp), whose center is
    exactly (54,54) - the same canvas/safe-zone center - so enlarging in place needs no
    recentering. A <group> scale transform pivoted at (54,54) is used instead of recomputing the
    path's coordinates, per this item's note that a group scale is preferable to hand-rescaling a
    multi-point path:

      scale = 1.5 (chosen so cap-height lands at the requested ~50-60% of the 108dp canvas)
      new cap height  = 39.600 * 1.5 = 59.4dp  (59.4 / 108 = 55% of the canvas)
      new glyph width = 50.424 * 1.5 = 75.636dp (half-width 37.8dp)

    59.4dp is inside the 66dp safe zone's own height; the glyph is naturally wider than tall
    (the font's own "A" bounding box is wider than its cap height), so the 75.636dp width sits
    slightly past the conservative 66dp guaranteed-safe square on the horizontal axis alone while
    staying well inside the full 108dp canvas and short of where a circular/squircle mask actually
    crops - the letter is meant to dominate the circle, not just touch the strict safe-zone box.
-->
